      "utf8": "money works. Scan the QR code on screen or click \nthe link in the description to get started. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the real market participants being forced to play \nalong with the flip-flop oil war does kind of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make sense. But the second big problem is that the \nspeculators who normally cushion this market have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kind of just given up. Derivative traders like to \nmake this sound more complicated than it is. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in normal times, these two groups do technically \neach have a role in this market. Actual oil  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suppliers and consumers who are happy to forego \nspeculative profits to avoid risk and locked in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guaranteed prices and speculators who are happy \nto accept the risk of fluctuating prices to try  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575120,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and make speculative profits. So this whole market \nis just what the finance bros like to call a risk  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transfer mechanism. Oil futures don't actually \nproduce anything. They are just a contract. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the only way to make money on them is to sell them \nfor a higher price than which they were bought.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Despite being a zero- sum game, this is still a \nmassive market with institutions that spend tens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596480,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of millions of dollars doing advanced research \nto find slight pricing discrepancies between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the market and reality. The classic example is \nthat some of these firms do crazy things like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604880,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "launch their own imaging satellites to track oil \nproduction, count the number of tankers sitting at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anchor, and monitor reflections off crude storage \ntanks to estimate how full they are. All to gain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a slight edge over the market. Other firms just go \nallin on trading speed. So they can react to news  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619680,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a thousandth of a second before everybody else \ndoes, which is also contributed to the market's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "knee-jerk reaction to news because it often is \na reaction that is processed before any real  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "humans actually realize what's going on. This \ncan be very lucrative, but that trading edge is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now being evaporated by the far less predictable \nannouncements surrounding this war. It's a little  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bit like someone counting cards to gain a slight \nadvantage at the blackjack table. If they are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very smart and collect the right data in a fair \ngame, they should be profitable over the long  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648800,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "term. But if the dealer is cheating by throwing \ncards to their buddy at the same table, well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then all that hard work is basically pointless. \nA desk of PhD analysts could synthesize terabytes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of proprietary data to ek out a few basis points \nof alpha in a normal market. But in this market,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all that effort can be wiped out by somebody who \ngot a heads up 15 minutes before a tweet went out.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, for the speculators, this is a zero sum \nmarket. For that uh miraculously timed trade to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "win, someone else has to lose. So, a lot of them \nhave just been choosing not to play. Typically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679600,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speculators are attracted to markets with a lot of \nvolatility. And this did happen at the beginning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the war as big swings meant big opportunities \nfor speculative profits. But trading firms have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understandably started seeing this as unacceptable \nrisk and have pulled back. According to data from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Saxo Bank, net speculative open interest has \ndropped towards a 16-year low with the only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exception of August last year at a time when \ntypically we would expect more market activity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705760,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, I know what you might be thinking. A bunch \nof hedge funds can't make money because the market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is rigged against them. Outside of glorious irony, \nwho really cares? Well, unfortunately this matters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because in theory these firms serve the function \nof price discovery and providing liquidity between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the suppliers and the users. They were obviously \ndoing this to seek their own profits. But in doing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so, they cushioned the market a bit. They absorbed \nsome of the volatility that would otherwise land  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "directly on the actual oil companies that just \nwanted to offload the risk of oil price swings.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733920,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without the risk accepting speculators, these \nactual participants are left absorbing more of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the price swing directly, which means the market \nhas less capacity to absorb shocks and and even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743280,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a moderately sized announcement can move prices \nfurther than it should because there are fewer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "counterparties willing to take the other side and \ncall these announcements out on their bluff. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the record, I just want to say that even with \nthe exodus, the vast majority of futures trading  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is still done by speculators. But the ratio of \nprice takers to risk acptors really matters.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Going from 2% to 5% of the market being physically \nsettled might only sound like a 3% difference.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in reality, it's actually more like a 150% \ndifference in market buffer. The third factor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that really needs to be acknowledged is that this \nwar has become very politically sensitive. There  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778240,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are really three regimes staking the reputation \non how much they can control the other two. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a big part of that control depends on where \nthis price line sits. All of that is to say  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that just as much as announcements around this \nwar have influenced movements in the price of oil,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "movements in the price of oil have also influenced \nthese announcements. So yeah, it sounds dumb and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it is dumb. But there is actually some good news \nin all of this. Despite everything I have just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "said about the market being more exposed, it \nis still slowly starting to ignore all of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dumb headlines. Obviously, there has been a \nlot of noise back and forth over this war,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and this is by no means going to be perfect, but \nas best as we could, we actually looked at all of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the major announcements we could find, saying the \nwar would be coming to an end and announcements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the war would be escalated and then lined \nthem up with the respective daily movements in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oil prices. And well, the impacts are clearly \ndeclining. Again, obviously, this isn't perfect.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I can only subject Harry to collecting so many \nuh data points as punishment for his crimes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the early days of the war, a tweet about \nbombing Iran back to the stone age made oil prices  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rise by 8%. In late June, there was a similar \npost about new strikes on Iran infrastructure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the market just totally ignored it. Prices \nactually fell slightly. On the flip side, peace  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853600,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talk announcements in April saw the biggest single \nday drop in oil prices outside of the pandemic.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858320,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But last month, the memorandum of understanding, \nthe one where we would give Iran a $300 billion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fund, only pushed prices down by 2%. In a \nsimilar reversal of logic, early this month,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an announcement of the Qatari deal going well \nalso actually pushed prices up. Now, obviously,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is not absolute, and real actions can still \npush prices around. Just this past Tuesday,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Iranian strikes on tankers sent Brent up 3% \nand then the ceasefire was declared over and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884640,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prices climbed again. But the general trend is \nthat the market is taking the words from both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sides less and less seriously with every false \nstart. Oh, and there is one more thing. Not only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the market taking these press releases less \nseriously, it also just seems to be taking the war  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less seriously as well. Despite no real progress \ntowards a long-term solution, prices are basically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right back where they were when this conflict \nstarted, which does raise the obvious question of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why? Well, this is the part of the video that has \nthe potential to really not age well. But for now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are a few reasons. One is that the world \nis simply just consuming less oil. People have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "responded to the increased prices by using fewer \nfossil fuels by an estimated 1 million barrels a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "day. China, in particular, the world's biggest net \nfossil fuel importer, has seen its crude imports  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drop to an 8-year low of roughly 7.8 million \nbarrels a day as of May. China has gone big on EVs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937760,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and alternative energy sources. Electric vehicles \nhit 62.9% of new car sales in China in May. And in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first week of June, that number hit a record \n66.7%. Among domestic Chinese brands, the EV share  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951120,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is now 81%. In China alone, EVs displaced roughly \n1 million barrels of oil demand per day in 2025,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that displacement is growing by about \n600,000 barrels per day every year. Cenek,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "China's biggest refiner, now says gasoline demand \nin China actually peaked back in 2023, and diesel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demand peaked in 2019. Chinese consumers are also \nmore price sensitive than richer households in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "West. So even if they don't have an EV, when oil \ngets expensive, they respond faster than a typical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "American family with a higher disposable income to \ntank the price bump. This trend with the biggest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985680,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oil consumer on the planet is scaring a lot of \nproducers into ramping up supplies and selling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now because if this shift can happen in China, \nthe rest of the world may not be far behind.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OPEC Plus has been raising output quotas, adding \nroughly 2.9 million barrels per day during 2025,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with another 800,000 approved so far this year \nand 188,000 more just approved for August. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007600,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "IEA is now predicting a surplus of 3.84 million \nbarrels per day this year, potentially approaching  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "4 million. The second thing to remember is that \nthe price being quoted here actually refers to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oil delivered to a specific location at a specific \ntime. A lot of the market is concerned that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buildup of oil stuck behind the straight could all \ncome out at once and lead to a massive spike in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supply, putting oil facilities above capacity, \neven if the oil would eventually find its way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to real consumers. There is also just speculation \nabout the midterms. Oil prices were one of the big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "factors in the last election, and both sides of \nthe conflict as well as both sides of the election  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043120,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know it. The government has not been subtle about \ntrying to bring prices down before voters start  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047680,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paying attention. The 60-day sanctions waiver \nallowing Iran to sell crude through August  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was widely described as having some uh domestic \npolitical advantages. Research from the Belelfer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Center found that every 1% increase in crude \noil prices 12 months before an election reduces  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "voter intention to reelect the incumbent party \nby roughly half a percentage point. On top of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of this, there is the fear of one of the most \noutlandish economic proposals I have ever heard,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that is that countries may just go ahead and \nshort the market themselves. It hasn't happened  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yet, and it may not happen at all, but it's so \ninteresting that it's worth talking about. About  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3 months ago, Japan floated the idea that it could \nuse its $1.4 trillion in foreign currency reserves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to short oil futures. So, why would they do \nthat? The idea is that its own currency is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "falling compared to the US dollar, and it wants \nto stabilize that slide. Almost all global oil  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trade is done in US dollars. By shorting oil \nprices down, it would theoretically mean people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104480,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would need fewer US dollars to settle their oil \ndeals, which would reduce demand for US dollars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in international forex markets, which would in \ntheory reduce the value of the US dollar relative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1113520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the yen. Now, yes, of course, a big short like \nthis means that in theory, they might be exposed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a GameStop style squeeze and be forced to \nbuy back all of that oil. But the thing is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1123920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they were going to buy it anyway. Japan imports \nmore than 95% of its crude from the Middle East.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when oil surges in dollar terms, Japan has \nto buy massive amounts of dollars just to pay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1134560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for its imports, which directly weakens the yen \neven further. Finance Minister Satsuki Katyama  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1140640,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blamed speculative crude oil futures moves for \ndisrupting exchange rates and said the government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1144800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is determined to take thorough action at all times \nand on all fronts. It's kind of insane. And again,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1150720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it hasn't actually happened. Honestly, the only \nreason I bring it up is because I thought it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1155280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was the most crazy hairbrain scheme I have ever \nseen seriously proposed on this kind of economic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1160240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scale. So, I wanted to yap about it. Now, if you \nwant further proof of just how long markets can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1165200,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ignore reality, go and watch this video next \nto find out why economic crashes never seem to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1169200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happen on schedule. And don't forget to like and \nsubscribe to keep on learning how money works."
    } ]
  } ]
}
